 % [before pervasiveness review E6, E12, E13, 2026-11-16] A hit also requires every branch that the graph marks resolved and that lies between the commit boundary and the oldest unresolved branch to have resolved before the transmission; a resolved branch on the wrong path is not checked, because the older unresolved branch already makes the transmitter speculative. Throughout this window the run matches the graph's commit boundary, and the instructions after a mispredicted branch stay transient until its squash lands (\S\ref{sec:bg-defenses}); STT, for example, delays their dependents ``until the mispredicted explicit branch B squashes''~\cite[\S 6.4.2]{yu2019stt}. What counts as a transmission is the transmission criterion of the defense's leakage contract (\S\ref{sec:leakage-predicate}), which we write from the defense's paper. For example, under STT's criterion a load transmits when it executes inside the window and obtains its data, by a cache access or a store-to-load forward, even if it is squashed later; the invisible-speculation defenses' criteria examine the structures each paper protects (\S\ref{sec:bg-defenses}): for InvisiSpec and SpecLFB, a speculative load transmits if it changes one of them (InvisiSpec's include the D-TLB); for CleanupSpec, which undoes such changes after a squash, a transmission is a change its cleanup leaves in place or a line its cleanup removes that no transient instruction installed. A run in which a forced prediction or hold did not take effect is invalid, never a hit. Print-only hooks log events that a build does not otherwise print, such as a branch's prediction and resolution.
